      <h4>Step 4: Setting Up Automated Documentation Updates</h4>

      <p>
        For an even more streamlined approach, you can automate architecture documentation updates using GitHub Actions:
      </p>

      <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-yaml">
name: Update Architecture Documentation

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]
    paths:
      - '**/*.csproj'
      - '**/*.sln'
      - 'docs/Architecture.md'

jobs:
  update-architecture:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      
      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
      
      # Conceptual step: run your custom architecture-update script here.
      # For example, a Python/Node script that calls an AI API to regenerate
      # docs/Architecture.md based on the current codebase structure.
      - name: Update architecture documentation
        run: |
          echo "Running custom architecture documentation update script..."
          # node scripts/update-architecture.js
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
      
      - name: Commit changes if needed
        run: |
          git config --local user.email "action@github.com"
          git config --local user.name "GitHub Action"
          git add docs/Architecture.md
          if git diff --staged --quiet; then
            echo "No changes to commit"
          else
            git commit -m "📝 Update architecture documentation"
            git push
          fi
      </CodeSnippet>

      <p>
        This workflow automatically triggers when project files change. The "Update architecture documentation" step
        is a placeholder for your own custom script—for example a Node.js or Python script that calls an AI API to
        regenerate <Highlight>docs/Architecture.md</Highlight> based on the current codebase.
      </p>

